[{:db/id 17592186078462 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-07e17262-4a40-5650-84e4-b70a5816bde7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-07e17262-4a40-5650-84e4-b70a5816bde7" :question/id #uuid "07e17262-4a40-5650-84e4-b70a5816bde7" :question/math-academy-id "q-182642" :question/problem "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all {{field-9}}." :question/worked-solution "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every natural number $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every natural number $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)=2(3)+3=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+6)(3-2)=9(1)=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=3,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)=(k+6)(k-2).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=(k+7)(k-1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & =(k+6)(k-2)+(2k+5) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+4k-12+2k+5 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+6k-7 \\\\  & =(k+7)(k-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all $n\\ge 3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85bada92-38f2-5036-a7ba-94276fe6d603" "answer-field-b73b909e-16c6-5fae-89a7-b2d740a870ea" "answer-field-e741aa16-fd6e-5a50-965b-57d1df24827c" "answer-field-3743059a-907e-58d0-9908-51c7af6ff964" "answer-field-b033408c-2472-5fda-87b2-82dd0d46e9bd" "answer-field-c383b528-ee6d-5442-a273-37c2705c7762" "answer-field-0135a5b6-1555-5496-9553-dd00774ec830" "answer-field-37799538-ef9b-5304-a883-8368e3d50a84" "answer-field-8deb77d0-2aa6-5b5d-8878-c55779e76ff3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85bada92-38f2-5036-a7ba-94276fe6d603" :answer-field/id #uuid "85bada92-38f2-5036-a7ba-94276fe6d603" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-ed0fcb59-4399-571c-a340-f4ddc4700f4e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-988e9b11-494e-57aa-95ac-62fae45c18fc" "answer-ed0fcb59-4399-571c-a340-f4ddc4700f4e" "answer-5179fa85-5800-5d8a-825e-19f954d6e613"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-988e9b11-494e-57aa-95ac-62fae45c18fc" :answer/id #uuid "988e9b11-494e-57aa-95ac-62fae45c18fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed0fcb59-4399-571c-a340-f4ddc4700f4e" :answer/id #uuid "ed0fcb59-4399-571c-a340-f4ddc4700f4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5179fa85-5800-5d8a-825e-19f954d6e613" :answer/id #uuid "5179fa85-5800-5d8a-825e-19f954d6e613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b73b909e-16c6-5fae-89a7-b2d740a870ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "b73b909e-16c6-5fae-89a7-b2d740a870ea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-769fa0d0-9eec-5b6c-888d-e3d9dc8a0dab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e9b4cae-a2e2-58fd-a6e4-d240bd78f305" "answer-bfc96098-3982-518d-8ef8-55cb6f8dfbc9" "answer-769fa0d0-9eec-5b6c-888d-e3d9dc8a0dab" "answer-fe6d86f6-5536-5cb