answer/id #uuid "7a12ee57-128d-5a8c-b973-54bd608fded3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k(k+7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8deb77d0-2aa6-5b5d-8878-c55779e76ff3" :answer-field/id #uuid "8deb77d0-2aa6-5b5d-8878-c55779e76ff3" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-eb8b37d8-fa8c-5fe9-b358-525dba301cc5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43e4a339-e05e-5b3b-99f5-a4b53b17019f" "answer-dbf5e2c2-4924-5765-9604-0061c5284d90" "answer-f9c59a23-9bfe-555c-82ed-db97d1cf8e64" "answer-eb8b37d8-fa8c-5fe9-b358-525dba301cc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43e4a339-e05e-5b3b-99f5-a4b53b17019f" :answer/id #uuid "43e4a339-e05e-5b3b-99f5-a4b53b17019f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbf5e2c2-4924-5765-9604-0061c5284d90" :answer/id #uuid "dbf5e2c2-4924-5765-9604-0061c5284d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n>3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9c59a23-9bfe-555c-82ed-db97d1cf8e64" :answer/id #uuid "f9c59a23-9bfe-555c-82ed-db97d1cf8e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n\\ge 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb8b37d8-fa8c-5fe9-b358-525dba301cc5" :answer/id #uuid "eb8b37d8-fa8c-5fe9-b358-525dba301cc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n\\ge 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078460 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fb1ce6e5-5388-5457-9a90-11e65945cce7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb1ce6e5-5388-5457-9a90-11e65945cce7" :question/id #uuid "fb1ce6e5-5388-5457-9a90-11e65945cce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-182681" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)=1(5-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)=k(5k-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3) & =(k+1)(5(k+1)-1) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5k+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e5313b3-1e44-53a0-a481-a7ddd62437df" "answer-field-07d618c1-0954-5940-96ab-f2c9b907b57b" "answer-field-0c2a71f1-9848-5d59-9301-5d0966d6363e" "answer-field-aa081afb-2dea-5ed6-9075-a6b452c575a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e5313b3-1e44-53a0-a481-a7ddd62437df" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5313b3-1e44-53a0-a481-a7ddd62437df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-f72f7ba8-1839-50e8-96e0-e9d4e93fafd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f72f7ba8-1839-50e8-96e0-e9d4e93fafd4" "answer-9fee694b-ef87-5821-a0dd-70fa6f197d4c" "answer-ec284fbf-ee23-5aa9-9248-ce2fa8331e5b" "answer-5c5d441f-226c-52dc-9953-f1572c3742a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f72f7ba8-1839-50e8-96e0-e9d4e93fafd4" :answer/id #uuid "f72f7ba8-1839-50e8-96e0-e9d4e93fafd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1(5-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fee694b-ef87-5821-a0dd-70fa6f197d4c" :answer/id #uuid "9fee694b-ef87-5821-a0dd-70fa6f197d4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(5-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec284fbf-ee23-5aa9-9248-ce2fa8331e5b" :answer/id #uuid "ec284fbf-ee23-5aa9-9248-ce2fa8331e5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(1)-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c5d441f-226c-52dc-9953-f1572c3742a2" :answer/id #uuid "5c5d441f-226c-52dc-9953-f1572c3742a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5